<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Bima Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1620 CE – 1958 CE (approx.) · capital Bima</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Muslim sultanate of eastern Sumbawa, a close ally of Makassar whose court chronicles and palace survived into the Indonesian republic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bima, the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bima-people">Mbojo</a> people, had been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> kingdom tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> before its rulers accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> under the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-gowa">Gowa Sultanate of Makassar</a>. Its first sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-kahir">Abdul Kahir</a>, took power around 1620 to 1640, and intermarriage with the Makassarese royal house bound the two courts closely together.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan ruled with a council of nobles and a hereditary chief minister, the Tureli Nggampo, while Islamic scholars advised on law. Bima exported horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, sappanwood and textiles, and its court kept a chronicle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bo-sangaji-kai">Bo Sangaji Kai</a>. After the fall of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gowa-sultanate">Makassar</a> it signed a treaty with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> in 1669.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The 1815 eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tambora">Mount Tambora</a> devastated the land and destroyed the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tambora">Kingdom of Tambora</a>. Bima remained a self-governing domain of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, and its last sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-salahuddin-of-bima">Muhammad Salahuddin</a>, brought it into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Its special status ended in 1958; the palace is now a museum.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bima-sultanate.jpg" alt="Bima Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: BayuAjisaka · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panggusu_Lima_Kesultanan_Bimaa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1620 CE – 1958 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bima</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abdul Kahir, Muhammad Salahuddin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bima (Mbojo)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Bima, Bima</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1789&amp;focus=bima-sultanate" aria-label="Bima Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7961 2494 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bima Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8281 2750L8312 2628M8281 2750L7959 2663M8281 2750L7959 2663M8281 2750L7959 2664M8281 2750L8273 2752"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8312 2628h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0M8273 2752h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8281 2750h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8281 2750h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bima. <a href="/atlas?year=1789&amp;focus=bima-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
